WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.662
STEPHANIE WONG: One
of the key tenets

00:00:01.662 --> 00:00:03.980
of scaling your
applications is automation.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.360
Will Critter Junction embrace
infrastructure as code?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.286
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.286 --> 00:00:10.238
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.590
In the last episode, we learned
about Critter Junction's

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.090
newfound popularity.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.292
While they've traditionally
run on premise,

00:00:21.292 --> 00:00:22.750
they've just started
port workloads

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.130
and rapidly spin up new
servers in the cloud.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.160
See, they're used to
the classic approach

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.460
to infrastructure creation,
which was to file a ticket.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.080
Somebody would long
into a management

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.990
portal and go through a
series of steps to provision

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.880
that piece of infrastructure.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.330
This works if you have a
relatively small footprint

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.040
or if the churn of
your infrastructure

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.980
is minimal, which was
usually the case with Critter

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.580
Junction's private data centers.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.930
A VM would live for months
to years with a limited scale

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.130
of deployment.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.090
But now with cloud
elasticity, VMs can and should

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:56.320
live shorter lives.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.470
They no longer have fixed costs
with depreciating hardware.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.760
Instead, you pay per
second, minute, or hour.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.440
The challenge here is delivering
a great user experience,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.060
even if demand spikes.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.700
Being able to rapidly spin
up resources and spin them

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.450
back down is a make it or break
it for both cost optimization

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.110
and user experience.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.890
But for Critter Junction,
manually filing tickets

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.930
and trying to reproduce
infrastructure at scale

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.320
has been time-consuming
and human-error-prone.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.540
If a spike occurs, it
could take hours or even

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.760
months to provision
infrastructure in time.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.040
Plus, it was hard to
track an audit changes.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.193
This is where Infrastructure
As Code, IAC, comes into play.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:36.610
It's a technique to treat your
infrastructure provisioning

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.530
and configuration
in the same way you

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.180
handle application code.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.600
You can automate provisioning of
your cloud resources with code,

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.700
create templates
for reproducibility,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.290
and store config files in
a source version control

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.920
so it's discoverable
and can be audited.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.320
Automating infrastructure
with a CI/CD pipeline

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.840
means any changes to
your configuration

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.080
can be automatically
tested and deployed.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.090
By adopting IAC,
Critter Junction

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.700
was able to boost
their ability to scale.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.550
Google Cloud deployment
manager is an IAC service

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.130
that automates the creation
and management of Google Cloud

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.310
resources using a declarative
yaml-based configuration

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:13.240
format.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.240
From dev test to prod, you can
build repeatable environments

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.720
with compute, networks,
storage, databases, users,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.660
and permissions using
simple configuration files.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.390
For more complex architectures
that you plan to reuse,

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.100
you can even break
your configuration

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.620
into templates, which
are a separate file that

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.270
defines a set of resources.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:33.500
You can reuse templates
across different deployments,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.610
which creates consistency
across complex deployments.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.530
In this example, this
configuration file

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.410
specifies resources from
Compute Engine and BigQuery

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.900
along with firewall
rules to provide access.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.650
Now Critter Junction can use the
G Cloud SDK to create, preview,

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.600
update, and delete deployments.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.430
Meanwhile, the
Google Cloud console

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.420
shows them all of their
deployed infrastructure

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.120
in a hierarchical view.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.780
Whether they need to do this one
time, 10 times, or 1,000 times,

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.820
this allows them to
have granular control

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.980
over the lifecycle
of their resources.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.110
For example, they were
able to reduce costs

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.170
by running a script every
morning that brings up hundreds

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.030
of machines and every evening
to use that same script to scale

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.370
it back down.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.460
And because no one likes drastic
changes to existing DevOps

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.270
workflows, you can use other
IAC tools like Terraform, Chef,

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.900
and Puppet to deploy resources
in Google Cloud and on prem

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:23.980
from one place.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.230
By removing manual steps
from their infrastructure

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:27.880
provisioning,
Critter Junction has

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.670
been able to get consistency
and speed of deployment

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.460
in their staging, QA, and
production environments

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.132
at scale.

00:03:34.132 --> 00:03:35.590
Stay tuned to follow
their journey.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.080
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

